---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import StemmaKey from '../components/StemmaKey.astro';
import { loadRecords, loadEdges } from '../lib/data';
import { drawFamily, drawFamilyNarrow } from '../lib/stemma-svg';
const { byId } = await loadRecords();
const edges = loadEdges();
const svg = drawFamily(byId, edges);
const narrow = drawFamilyNarrow(byId, edges);
---
<Base title="The stemma" description="The LLaMA family of language models drawn as a manuscript stemma: descent, training data, and contamination, each line sourced.">
  <p class="eyebrow">The stemma · first family</p>
  <h1>The LLaMA family, drawn as a stemma</h1>
  <p>
    Textual critics draw the family tree of a text’s manuscripts as a <em>stemma</em>: the oldest copies at the top, each
    copy below the one it was made from. This is the same drawing for one family of language models, from LLaMA (February
    2023) through Llama 2, Code Llama, and the chat models fine-tuned from them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Time runs down the page. The two hollow circles at the top left are closed models: their weights were never
    published, so, like a lost manuscript, they are known only through what descends from them. The dashed lines are
    what stemmatics calls <strong>contamination</strong>: text written by those closed models became the training data
    for Alpaca and Vicuna, so those open models draw on two lines at once.
  </p>
  <div class="only-wide figure-wide" set:html={svg} />
  <div class="only-narrow">
    <div class="figure-narrow" set:html={narrow} />
    <p class="muted src">
      On a phone the families are drawn as one indented column: each model sits under the model it came from, siblings
      in date order. The closed models and datasets sit in the left margin, and their lines run into each model that
      trained on them.
    </p>
    <details class="full-drawing">
      <summary>Show the full drawing (scrolls sideways)</summary>
      <div class="figure-wide" set:html={svg} />
    </details>
  </div>
  <p class="muted">Every line is a recorded edge and links to its source; every node links to its record.</p>
  <h2>Key</h2>
  <StemmaKey />
  <h2>How to read it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A node sits at its release date. A child is never drawn level with its parent, so a few nodes sit slightly below
      their true month; the date under each name is the recorded one.</li>
    <li>ShareGPT has no recorded release date, so it is placed just above the first model trained on it.</li>
    <li>Line form tells you the relation; colour tells you the evidence. The full definitions are in the
      <a href="/method/#relations">method</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>What this drawing is not</h2>
  <p>
    It is one family, laid out by hand, as a test of the drawing conventions. The whole network (every record, laid out
    from the data) is the next step. The data behind it is already public:
    <a href="/data/edges/edges.jsonl">edges.jsonl</a> and the <a href="/records/">records</a>.
  </p>
</Base>
